  "why": "The statement asks for f(n) for every n, so an explicit 2n configuration at a given n determines f(n) exactly there (pigeonhole gives the matching upper bound). That settles a restriction of the question, so I scored these as case rather than instances: instances is for spot-checking a universal assertion, and these events determine the asked-for value for those n. The configurations are explicit and can be checked with a trivial collinearity test, so they are checkable even where the only record is the maintainer's log. Census entries at n already settled (Riley, Prellberg's symmetry-class censuses, Davies, Kudriashov's rot2 additions) move no value and are instances. The MCTS paper's ~1.8n configurations for 82–119 raise the lower bounds there, so it is a bound, but doubtful. Its search uses no learned model, so its AI role is none. Kudriashov's paper is generated by its own disclosure, but its planar content is a heuristic constant with no bound, so its scope is none. Ramanathan et al. only reproduced known optima, so none. The Putterman construction is AI-generated but concerns infinite sets, so it is a variant.",
